# Copy to .env and `chmod 600 .env`. Never commit .env.
# Keep a copy in your password manager: with this file and the two repos you can
# rebuild everything (see README, "Disaster recovery").
# Wrap values in single quotes so characters like $ or # are taken literally.
# Don't use | or , in these passwords: nebula-sync uses them as separators.

# --- Secrets ---

# Web/API password of the primary Pi-hole (the Raspberry Pi)
PI_PRIMARY_PASSWORD='change-me'

# Web/API password of the replica Pi-hole (the container on this Mac)
PIHOLE_WEB_PASSWORD='change-me'

# ntfy.sh topic for pi-monitor alerts (Pi-hole down / back). Anyone who knows
# the name can read and post to it, so make it long and random:
#   echo "pihole-$(openssl rand -hex 12)"
# Subscribe to the same topic in the ntfy app (server: ntfy.sh).
NTFY_TOPIC='change-me'

# --- Site settings (not secret, but they describe your network) ---

# Fixed LAN address of the Raspberry Pi (primary). Give it a DHCP reservation.
PI_IP='192.168.1.2'

# Fixed LAN address of this Mac (replica). DNS binds to this address only.
REPLICA_IP='192.168.1.3'

# Port of the replica's web UI: http://<REPLICA_IP>:<REPLICA_WEB_PORT>/admin
REPLICA_WEB_PORT='8081'

# Where `scripts/export-pi-config.sh --teleporter` saves full backups. They hold
# secrets (password hash), so keep this outside the repo and outside local/.
BACKUP_DIR="$HOME/pihole-backups"

# Time zone for log timestamps. TZ is a tz database name; TZ_POSIX is the same zone as a
# POSIX rule for pi-monitor (its alpine image has no tz database). Default: UTC.
# Example: TZ='America/New_York' and TZ_POSIX='EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0'
TZ='Etc/UTC'
TZ_POSIX='UTC0'
